ated. Therefore, the patient is at greatest risk for coronary artery aneurysms without timely and appropriate treatment. Other complications such as hemolytic anemia, encephalitis, mitral valve regurgitation, and minimal change disease are not primary or significant in Kawasaki disease and can be ruled out as the primary concern. Prompt medical intervention is crucial to mitigate the risk of these severe cardiovascular complications. \n"], "raw_output": "Option: B", "error": null}
{"question": "A 32-year-old man presents to the emergency department with fever, nausea, and vomiting. The patient states that his symptoms started last night and have persisted to this morning. He has a past medical history of asthma, and his current medications include albuterol and fluticasone. His temperature is 102\u00b0F (38.9\u00b0C), blood pressure is 110/60 mmHg, pulse is 95/min, respirations are 17/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. On physical exam, the patient seems uncomfortable. He requests that the lights in the room stay turned off during the physical exam. The patient's cardiac exam demonstrates a normal rate and rhythm, and his pulmonary exam is clear to auscultation bilaterally. Musculoskeletal exam reveals diminished mobility of the neck in all four directions, with flexion of the neck causing the patient discomfort. Lumbar puncture is performed and results are seen below.\n\nCerebrospinal fluid (CSF):\nCell count: 175/mm^3\nCl-: 119 mEq/L\nGlucose: 49 mg/dL\nPressure: 150 mmH2O\nTotal protein: 55 mg/dL\n\nWhich of the following is the best treatment for this patient's most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acyclovir", "B": "Amphotericin and 5-flucytosine", "C": "Ceftriaxone", "D": "Ceftriaxone and vancomycin", "E": "IV fluids and monitoring"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Neurology", "Infectious Disease", "Internal Medicine", "Pediatrics"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Infectious Disease"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The patient's presentation of fever, nausea, vomiting, and neck stiffness, along with the CSF findings, strongly suggests a diagnosis of bacterial meningitis. The CSF analysis shows an elevated white blood cell count (175/mm\u00b3), low glucose (49 mg/dL), and elevated protein (55 mg/dL), which are typical findings in bacterial meningitis. Additionally, the patient's discomfort with neck movement and the elevated CSF opening pressure (150 mm